<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maitraka dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 475–776</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Maitrakas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maitraka dynasty ruled western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from about 475 to 776 from their capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valabhi">Valabhi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a>, now Vala in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhatarka">Bhatarka</a>, a general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>, they became independent as Gupta power faded. Valabhi was a renowned centre of Buddhist and Jain learning, where the Jain canon was written down at a council in the 5th century. The dynasty fell to Arab raids from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maitraka-dynasty.jpg" alt="Maitraka dynasty" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Valabhi_(Saurashtra)_drachms._Late_5th-8th_century_Capped_head_right_in_Ksatrapa_style_Trident;_Brahmi_legend_around.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
